  "worked_solution": "Let's reverse the limits of integration on the known result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{9}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{9}^{4}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can split up the given integral using the sum rule and factor out the constants. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{9}(4f(x)+2)\\,\\text{d}x & =4{\\int }_{4}^{9}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+2{\\int }_{4}^{9}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4(-6)+2\\cdot x\\,{|}_{4}^{9} \\\\  & =-24+2(9-4) \\\\  & =-24+10 \\\\  & =-14.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
